---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `[42, 17, 93, 8, 55, 31]

Look at everything. The smallest is 8.
Put it at the front, and move 42 to where 8 was.

[8, 17, 93, 42, 55, 31]
 ^^ done. Never look at it again.

Now do the same to what is left.`;

const Figure2 = `let smallest = i;

for (let j = i + 1; j < list.length; j++) {
  if (list[j] < list[smallest]) {
    smallest = j;
  }
}`;

const Figure3 = `let temp = list[i];
list[i] = list[smallest];
list[smallest] = temp;`;

const Figure4 = `list[i] = list[smallest];
list[smallest] = list[i];   // both are now the same value`;

const Figure5 = `n         binary    linear    sort
8         4         8         28
16        5         16        120
32        6         32        496
1000000   20        1000000   499999500000`;

const Figure6 = `Double the list.

  binary search    one more step
  linear search    twice the steps
  selection sort   about four times the steps`;

const Figure7 = `n     doubling
8     256
16    65536
32    4294967296
64    18446744073709551616`;

const Task1 = `let scores = [42, 17, 93, 8, 55, 31];

function selectionSort(list) {
  let comparisons = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < list.length - 1; i++) {
    let smallest = i;

    for (let j = i + 1; j < list.length; j++) {
      comparisons = comparisons + 1;

      if (list[j] < list[smallest]) {
        smallest = j;
      }
    }

    let temp = list[i];
    list[i] = list[smallest];
    list[smallest] = temp;

    console.log("After pass " + (i + 1) + ": " + list);
  }

  console.log("Comparisons: " + comparisons);
  return list;
}

selectionSort(scores);

console.log("");
console.log("--- a list that is already sorted ---");
selectionSort([8, 17, 31, 42, 55, 93]);
`;

const Task2 = `function halvings(n) {
  let steps = 0;
  let left = n;

  while (left > 0) {
    steps = steps + 1;
    left = Math.floor(left / 2);
  }

  return steps;
}

function pairs(n) {
  return (n * (n - 1)) / 2;
}

function doubling(n) {
  let total = 1;

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    total = total * 2;
  }

  return total;
}

let sizes = [8, 16, 32, 64];

console.log("n\\tbinary\\tlinear\\tsort\\tdoubling");

for (let i = 0; i < sizes.length; i++) {
  let n = sizes[i];
  console.log(n + "\\t" + halvings(n) + "\\t" + n + "\\t" + pairs(n) + "\\t" + doubling(n));
}
`;

const Task3 = `// A computer does one billion steps per second.
//
// For each of these, work out roughly how long the job takes
//   and write the answer in a comment:
//
//   1. Linear search through 1 billion records.
//   2. Binary search through 1 billion sorted records.
//   3. Selection sort on 1 million records.
//   4. A doubling algorithm on an input of 64.
//
// One of these four does not finish in your lifetime.
//   Say which, and by how much you missed.
`;

const ExampleOutput = `After pass 1: 8,17,93,42,55,31
After pass 2: 8,17,93,42,55,31
After pass 3: 8,17,31,42,55,93
After pass 4: 8,17,31,42,55,93
After pass 5: 8,17,31,42,55,93
Comparisons: 15

--- a list that is already sorted ---
After pass 1: 8,17,31,42,55,93
After pass 2: 8,17,31,42,55,93
After pass 3: 8,17,31,42,55,93
After pass 4: 8,17,31,42,55,93
After pass 5: 8,17,31,42,55,93
Comparisons: 15`;

const TableOutput = `n	binary	linear	sort	doubling
8	4	8	28	256
16	5	16	120	65536
32	6	32	496	4294967296
64	7	64	2016	18446744073709552000`;

const NotationJs = `let temp = list[i];
list[i] = list[smallest];
list[smallest] = temp;`;

const NotationPseudo = `temp ← aList[i]
aList[i] ← aList[smallest]
aList[smallest] ← temp`;

const NotationCount = `i ← 1
REPEAT UNTIL(i > n)
{
  j ← 1
  REPEAT UNTIL(j > n)
  {
    <one step>
    j ← j + 1
  }
  i ← i + 1
}`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 2.12: Sorting and Algorithmic Efficiency</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 2.12</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Sorting and Algorithmic Efficiency</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Sorting as a Cost</p>
              <p>What Happens When the Input Grows</p>
              <p>Reasonable and Unreasonable Time</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Why Bother Sorting</h2>
              <p>Last session: binary search needs a sorted list.</p>
              <p class="fragment">So somebody has to sort it.</p>
              <p class="fragment">And that costs something. Today is about what.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Watch Three of Them Run</h2>
              <p>Same list. Same finished order.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Completely different behavior on the way there.</p>
              <p class="fragment">And a different amount of work.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>One of Them, in Code</h2>
              <p>Find the smallest. Move it to the front. Repeat on the rest.</p>
              <Code code={Figure1} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Selection Sort" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">
                <b class="text-sn-theme">No sorting algorithm is on the exam.</b> This one
                is here because counting its steps is.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Finding the Smallest</h2>
              <Code code={Figure2} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="The Inner Loop" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">This is Algorithm A from 2.9, running on part of the list.</p>
              <p class="fragment">It tracks the <b>index</b>, not the value. You need to know where it was.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Swap Needs Three Lines</h2>
              <Code code={Figure3} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Three Lines, Not Two" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <Code code={Figure4} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="The Broken Version" showLineNumbers={false} class="fragment" />
              <p class="fragment">The first line overwrites the value the second line needs.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><code>temp</code> exists to hold it for exactly one line.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Now Watch What It Does Not Know</h2>
              <p>Some passes change nothing at all.</p>
              <p class="fragment">The list can be finished with three passes left to run.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Selection sort has no way to notice.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Which Means</h2>
              <p>An already-sorted list costs exactly the same as a scrambled one.</p>
              <p class="fragment">15 comparisons either way, on six items.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>No best case.</b> Contrast that with linear search, which got faster when it got lucky.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>That Number Is the Real Subject</h2>
              <p>We have been counting comparisons for three sessions.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Not seconds &mdash; a fast computer makes a bad algorithm look fine until the input grows.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Steps as the input grows</b> is the measurement.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Everything You Have Written This Unit</h2>
              <Code code={Figure5} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Steps for a List of n" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Look at the bottom row.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Shape Matters, Not the Number</h2>
              <Code code={Figure6} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Double the Input" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">One more step. Twice. Four times.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Those three answers are the whole subject.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Where the Shapes Come From</h2>
              <p><b>Halving</b> the problem each pass &rarr; one more step per doubling.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>One loop</b> over the list &rarr; steps grow with <code>n</code>.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>A loop inside a loop</b> &rarr; steps grow with <code>n</code> squared. That is the sort.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Then There Is This</h2>
              <Code code={Figure7} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Doubling" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Every <code>+1</code> to the input <b>doubles</b> the work.</p>
              <p class="fragment">At n = 64, a billion steps a second takes <b>585 years</b>.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Two Categories</h2>
              <p><b>Reasonable time:</b> <b>polynomial or lower</b> &mdash; constant, halving, linear, square, cube.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Unreasonable time:</b> <b>exponential</b> or <b>factorial</b>.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Binary search is on the reasonable side, and it is faster than linear. <i>Or lower</i> is doing real work in that sentence.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Read That Boundary Carefully</h2>
              <p><code>n</code> cubed on a million items is a very long time. It is still called reasonable.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Reasonable: buy a machine 1000&times; faster, solve a much bigger problem.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Unreasonable: buy a machine 1000&times; faster, and handle about <b>ten more items</b>.</p>
              <p class="fragment">The line is not about being fast. It is about whether hardware can ever rescue you.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Describing selection sort, and why a swap needs three assignments</li>
                <li>Measuring an algorithm by counting how many times a statement executes</li>
                <li>Showing that two correct algorithms for one problem can cost differently</li>
                <li>Distinguishing reasonable from unreasonable running time</li>
                <li>Explaining why a faster computer does not fix an unreasonable algorithm</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Selection sort</dt>
                  <dd>Repeatedly select the smallest remaining item and place it next.</dd>
                  <dt>Swap</dt>
                  <dd>Exchange two values, using a third variable so neither is lost.</dd>
                  <dt>Efficiency</dt>
                  <dd>An estimate of the computational resources an algorithm uses, as a function of the size of the input.</dd>
                  <dt>Reasonable time</dt>
                  <dd>Polynomial efficiency or lower &mdash; constant, halving, linear, square, cube.</dd>
                  <dt>Unreasonable time</dt>
                  <dd>Exponential or factorial efficiency.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Create a new <b>JavaScript program</b> named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">2-12-efficiency</b>.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: What a Sort Costs</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Write the code below and run it.</li>
            <li>
              <b>Three of the five passes on the scrambled list change nothing.</b>{" "}
              Find them, and say in a comment why the list did not move.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Then compare the two comparison counts.</b> Record both. Linear
              search got faster when it got lucky &mdash; say why this sort does
              not.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <Code code={Task1} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Sorting" class="mt-3" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: From One Sort to Every Algorithm</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Start a new file and write the code below.</li>
            <li>
              Look down each column as <code>n</code> doubles.{" "}
              <b>Write in a comment what happens to each one</b>, in words rather
              than numbers.
            </li>
            <li>
              The <code>sort</code> column and the <code>doubling</code> column
              both look big at n = 8. Say which you would rather be stuck with at
              n = 1000, and why the answer is obvious once you look at n = 64.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <Code code={Task2} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Efficiency" class="mt-3" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Put a Time on It</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Fill in the comment block.</li>
          </ul>
          <Code code={Task3} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Efficiency" class="mt-3" />

          <h5 class="mt-4">Challenge (optional)</h5>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Print <code>doubling(64)</code> and compare its last few digits
              against the value on the slide. <b>They do not match.</b> Explain
              what happened, using what you know from Activity 1.2 about how many
              bits a number gets.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Exam Notation -->
        <section id="exam-notation">
          <h2>Same Idea, Exam Notation</h2>

          <div class="row g-3 my-4">
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={NotationJs}
                frame="code"
                lang="javascript"
                title="JavaScript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={NotationPseudo}
                frame="code"
                lang="txt"
                title="Exam Notation"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The swap is one of the few places where the two notations line up
            almost exactly. <code>aList[i] ← aList[j]</code> is written on the
            reference sheet as its own list operation, so the exam expects you to
            read it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">But one thing does not line up at all</h3>

          <p>
            On the reference sheet, <code>aList ← bList</code> assigns a{" "}
            <b>copy</b> of the list. In JavaScript it does not &mdash; it makes a
            second name for the same list, and sorting through one name changes
            what you see through the other.
          </p>

          <p>
            So a sort question in exam notation can hand you two list names and
            expect the original to be untouched. <b>The same question in
            JavaScript would have a different answer.</b> This is the single
            biggest mismatch between the two notations in the whole course.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">Counting steps in notation</h3>

          <Code
            code={NotationCount}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="A Loop Inside a Loop"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b>Counting steps is an exam skill, and it usually arrives as a
            nested loop like this one.</b> The question is almost always the same:
            how many times does the inner statement run?
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <b>Read the bounds, not the shape.</b> Two nested loops that each
              run <code>n</code> times give <code>n</code> squared steps. Two
              nested loops where the inner one runs 3 times give <code>3n</code>{" "}
              &mdash; which grows like <code>n</code>, not like <code>n</code>{" "}
              squared.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b><code>REPEAT n TIMES</code> gives you no counter.</b> When you
              need the index you keep it yourself, which is why the notation is
              longer than the JavaScript.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b>And one thing you will not be asked for.</b> There is a formal
            notation for describing efficiency, and it is explicitly out of scope
            for this course and this exam. <i>Twice the input, four times the
            work</i> is the level of answer the exam wants.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Task 1 should produce exactly this.</p>

          <Code
            code={ExampleOutput}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            title="Task 1 Output"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">And Task 2 this.</p>

          <Code
            code={TableOutput}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            title="Task 2 Output"
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            The columns are separated by tabs, so they may line up differently
            depending on your screen. <b>The last number is worth a second
            look</b> &mdash; see the optional challenge.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>Sorting six items took 15 comparisons. Binary search on six items takes about three. When is it worth paying the 15?</li>
            <li>Selection sort cannot tell that it has finished early. Describe, in words, what you would have to add so it could.</li>
            <li>An algorithm taking <code>n</code> cubed steps is called reasonable, and one that doubles is called unreasonable. Why is the line drawn between those two rather than somewhere else?</li>
            <li>Your computer gets a thousand times faster overnight. Which of the four columns benefits least, and why?</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit the required files to the appropriate dropbox.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
